import type { V205BookData } from '../types';

export const DATA: V205BookData = {
  book: "Philippians",
  display: "Philippians",
  chapters: {
    "1": {"chapter":1,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul had a very special care for every church that was started under his watch, and were willing to go the distance for Christ. Most of the churches he helped start were churches run or enveloped by Gentile believers, and the Jews of his day HATED that. They couldn't stand Paul's message of salvation through Christ for the world. So they constantly tried to stone him, stop him, keep him quiet, and all it did was make his message more powerful and more meaningful."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have studied through several letters from Paul and we have noticed that Paul has a lot of very choice words for these individual churches. The letter we read just before this one was to the Ephesian church, and they had a lot of growing up to do. Most churches have a lot of growing up to do. Even this church of Philippi, we will see that there are some growth pains that need to be addressed and taken care of. I do feel, however, that Paul has a very special interest in this church above some of the others, and they seem to be doing a lot of things right and heading down the right course. They were just missing some very key ingredients that would help them maintain that course if they are willing to take Paul's advice on the matter."},{"kind":"para","text":"The things that we are about to read in this chapter (like any other chapter) are heavily influential to our Christian lifestyle today. If we want to grow in our faith, and in our walks, Paul has some very useful and practical tools in this letter that will get us there. Honestly, these things should be something we automatically know as we become Christians. There are certain things that the Holy Spirit of God will just work into the lifestyle of a Christian that no one else even has to tell them to do, they just know that they should be listening to it and following it."},{"kind":"para","text":"These letters are useful, however, because we can sometimes be so entrenched with the world around us that we lose sight of our ultimate goal (which is Jesus Christ, and being present with the Lord while we are here on Earth). The Philippians were a heavenly-minded folk, which is great (and we all should be), but they could not (like we all do) forget that there was still work to be done here on Earth."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"Paul and Timotheus","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are assured that this is Paul writing this letter. If you aren’t confident with who is writing the letters when it factually states the name, we will not get very far into understanding the rest of this book. We will also notice that Paul is taking Timothy with him on this campaign to write to the Philippians. Timothy is a man that Paul desired to heavily influence. He was Paul’s right-hand man. I think it’s good to have someone under you that you can pour into. It wasn’t always just Timothy, though, that Paul was influencing. He had several people around him that he was pouring into (Timothy was just a bit closer to Paul)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Aristarchus"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Aristarchus my fellowprisoner saluteth you, and Marcus, sister's son to Barnabas, (touching whom ye received commandments: if he come unto you, receive him;)","ref":"Colossians 4:10"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Marcus, Aristarchus, Demas, Lucas, my fellowlabourers.","ref":"Philemon 1:24"},{"kind":"para","text":"Barnabas"},{"kind":"para","text":"I think Barnabas was a little more of an influence on Paul than it was the opposite way, but they still had times of sharing in the joy of ministry and building one another up."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And when James, Cephas, and John, who seemed to be pillars, perceived the grace that was given unto me, they gave to me and Barnabas the right hands of fellowship; that we should go unto the heathen, and they unto the circumcision. Only they would that we should remember the poor; the same which I also was forward to do.","ref":"Galatians 2:9-10"},{"kind":"para","text":"Epaphras"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"As ye also learned of Epaphras our dear fellowservant, who is for you a faithful minister of Christ;","ref":"Colossians 1:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"Gaius"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Gaius mine host, and of the whole church, saluteth you. Erastus the chamberlain of the city saluteth you, and Quartus a brother.","ref":"Romans 16:23"},{"kind":"para","text":"Time permits me to speak about Jason, Sopater, John Mark (who was more influenced by Peter, but there was a time when Paul and Mark traveled together), Luke, Onesimus, Silas, Sosthenes, Trophimus, Tychicus, and others. There were seasons, obviously, when Paul would have other men around him who would not just help him in the ministry but whom he would keep around to build fellowship, break bread, and raise up for the gospel. These were not just people Paul could use; they were people Paul loved."},{"kind":"para","text":"These were serious people who were serious about the ministry and serious about Paul’s vision going forward. They understood what Paul desired and understood the calling of Christ. They loved Paul, and Paul loved them. Timothy is certainly a special case, but he was not the only one that Paul developed. Paul makes it clear that he is writing to the church at Philippi, and he is writing specifically to the leadership."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I’m thankful that Paul starts his letters off this way. He doesn’t just unload on churches; he extends grace and peace to them first. Even if the church doesn’t deserve grace or peace, he still wants to extend that to them because he knows what a church needs."},{"kind":"para","text":"Can I just be blunt here for a moment? If you are a leader today, you may think you know what the church needs. Perhaps the church needs the things you desire to see it grow. Maybe the church needs a bigger auditorium, or a new building. Maybe the church needs a nicer place of fellowship, and some amenities that will help people desire and appreciate fellowship. Maybe the church needs four hundred life groups and thirty Bible studies to help really drive home the message of the gospel. Maybe it needs its potlucks, events, food trucks, and the sorts to make people feel welcome and at home. But really, what the church ultimately needs above and beyond all that is grace and peace."},{"kind":"para","text":"I see a lot of churches being built up for great things, but I don’t find many (if any) of them doing it with grace or with peace. Some might be doing it with grace, but they certainly have a congregation that is not at peace. Some might be doing it with peace (probably more for personal gain), but they don’t have grace while they are doing it. Others don’t have either one, and they are just going with the flow and observing the times. Grace and peace are something that leaders should be building up for their congregants. Otherwise, we will have a lot of people (including in the leadership) feeling disjointed and disconnected. A growing church must look to provide as much grace and peace as possible. We must remember this prayer to the Philippians as we read through this letter."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":11,"title":"A Great Church","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a statement from Paul here! Whenever Paul thinks about this church, he thanks the Lord for them. I can’t think of another church that Paul has said quite those words to. If anything, Paul has reminded the people of the other churches to give thanks, rather than speaking about how thankful he is for those churches. Paul really loves this church, and he makes it very personal right off the bat. He says, “I thank MY God…” It’s their God too, mind you, but that is the picture he is painting here. They share the same God."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have reason to believe that these people, here, are doing things quite well, but there is still something that is missing in their congregation. It’s something that most churches are missing today, and it’s really the theme of Philippians. Paul is actually quite masterful at bringing it up without shoving it in their faces or making them feel like they are such failures. Some of these other churches might come away from Paul’s letters feeling like total failures, but this letter shows a little bit of a different side of Paul. It’s a church that had its head on straight, but the grace and peace part is really what we will be focusing on throughout the rest of this letter."}]},{"start":4,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I have quite a lengthy prayer list to go through. On some days, I am deep in prayer and make sure to hit every soul written down. On other days, I lose track of time and must rush through praying for individuals that I know need the prayer the most right now. Don’t we all need it in urgency? I imagine I am not the only one. I have a lot of things and people to pray for, with my family being at the top of that list, and not enough time in the day to do much of anything else. If I could, I would be on my knees 24/7 for these people. Paul tells the church at Thessalonica that every occasion is an occasion to pray."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Pray without ceasing.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 5:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"Notice, however, that Paul says he is “ALWAYS” making prayer (every time he prays) for this church in Philippi. I assume he is hearing the affairs of the Philippian church and it brings a smile to his face. Truly, there are not many churches during Paul’s day that were quite right. He is happy to see a church taking his advice in strides and actually following through with it. It proves that the structure of the church works if they are willing to keep the focus on Christ. Too many churches that Paul starts were deviating from the course, and that is why many of these letters existed."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sadly, we could take an account of churches today and find the same problem. We have twenty-two letters, all spread out to about thirty churches (as an estimate, some of these letters address several churches, like Revelation and 1, 2, 3 John), and there are only (perhaps) two churches out of the thirty that have a positive review. This being one of them. We can have thirty churches in one area of Lancaster (or Palmdale) and get the same statistic. Even if we had all the same doctrine at the start, we will see that many leaders of the churches (and the congregants themselves) will allow sin to slip through those doors and become a part of their culture."},{"kind":"para","text":"This church at Philippi was a special church, and it started strong and continued strong. They are people who seem to take special care of Paul as well, because they continuously keep him up to date with the things that are happening in the church. I don’t know how they kept him up to date, but I assume Paul had someone to check on their affairs as often as possible so he knows how to address their situation and help them change course. We will also see (in the next chapter) that there is a man from the church at Philippi coming to him for this specific letter."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, I know that this verse and verse seven go together, but I cannot just let this verse slide by without diving deeper into it. It’s such a good verse, and it’s one that we should all take to heart personally. This is a good reminder to all of us. We will go through moments in life where we will feel somewhat forgotten and disconnected. We will (sadly) feel like an afterthought, even from the people in your church home. You will be listed as “the cleanup” crew. You are only ever called upon to “clean up” a very bleak and hurt situation. I have no problem with cleaning up, don’t get me wrong. I am not too proud to take a mop and bucket and clean up something that needs to be cleaned. I like the saying, “See the need, take the lead.” That is something we should all be striving for in our daily lives."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, there are going to be times (and this happens far more often to me than I like) where you will only be looked at as someone who can clean up a mess someone else made. Whether that is a literal mess or a spiritual one, it doesn’t matter. You are the cleanup guy. It is not a fun job to have. You go in with the thought that you will get to sit and soak in the word of God, and instead, you are in the back of the auditorium cleaning up this mess or that mess. Let’s take this into a spiritual application (because I find it happens far more spiritually than it does physically)."},{"kind":"para","text":"You are under the impression that you are going to a meeting in order for you to be poured into, and you come to find out that the only one doing the pouring is yourself. You pour yourself out, and you think, “Well, I will just have a conversation with so and so, and that will refresh me.” It doesn’t, though. You meet up with the next person, and you are doing some spiritual cleaning in their life as well. Your cleaning water starts to look a little murky. It’s time for some refreshing water, and so the next person you feel will be the one. Guess what… they use you up as well. Perhaps you even go to your pastor to speak about it, and it’s the same ball game. There is no one else in the church willing to pour into you, but they are all certainly willing to abuse your pouring out."},{"kind":"para","text":"Let’s take a very close look at this verse, however. “Being confident” means we can know for sure. It’s not a guess as to what will happen; it’s a peace that comes over us because we are assured. We are assured of what? It says we can be sure that God (that is the one that has begun a good work in you) will perform that good work “until the day of Jesus Christ…” What day is that? Well, I don’t know, but it certainly feels like it could be any day now. Perhaps that day will only come the day you die. That doesn’t sound very exciting at face value, but when you dive deeper into Paul’s words here, we see that he is saying this to produce joy."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now the God of hope fill you with all joy and peace in believing, that ye may abound in hope, through the power of the Holy Ghost.","ref":"Romans 15:13"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Hitherto have ye asked nothing in my name: ask, and ye shall receive, that your joy may be full.","ref":"John 16:24"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Whom having not seen, ye love; in whom, though now ye see him not, yet believing, ye rejoice with joy unspeakable and full of glory:","ref":"1 Peter 1:8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"A merry heart doeth good like a medicine: but a broken spirit drieth the bones.","ref":"Proverbs 17:22"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And ye now therefore have sorrow: but I will see you again, and your heart shall rejoice, and your joy no man taketh from you.","ref":"John 16:22"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Rejoice evermore.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 5:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a very important note to make because we will all have a season of being used up. I completely understand your situation. But Paul, inspired by the Holy Spirit, says, “Hey, God started a good work in you, and He will complete that good work in you. He isn’t going to just stop.” That is, in itself, a reason to rejoice right there. Does this mean we should continue to just be used up by everyone around us? No, that is not what I’m saying. What I’m saying is, though everyone around you may not be working in your favor, the Lord certainly is. He will not give up pouring into you."},{"kind":"para","text":"Here’s what I will say (because I don’t want this to be misconstrued that we should just constantly be seeking to be poured into by everyone else): God will pour into you, and it is our jobs (as Christians) to pour out into others. Christians who seek to be poured into more than pouring out are baby Christians. They are not grown. That includes even in our church leadership. We must be willing to be a “pouring out” type of people. All of us. All the time."},{"kind":"para","text":"We also must be willing to be a “pouring in” type of people. We must be intentionally seeking times away from others to specifically spend time with the Lord and be poured into. The church should be willing to give its leaders and laypeople time for that. They should be looking for times to do specifically that. In fact, (and I might get in trouble here), the church should be catering towards that for their congregation. They should be teaching and nurturing people to spend more time with their families and less time in other people’s families. I don’t see that happening often in the church, though. So, we get into a mode of just pouring out and never have time to be poured into (even when the church has an off season). That is something else Paul will address later."},{"kind":"para","text":"Our ultimate desire and goal is to know God and spend time with Him. That is a good work to have. Spurgeon said, “To bring a man from darkness into light is good. This good is the best that a man can receive. To make a man healthy in body and wealthy in estate, to educate his mind and train his faculties; all these are good, but in comparison with the salvation of the soul, they sink into insignificance.” Being poured into by the Lord is a good work, and it is the only way we can have any other good work pouring out of us."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This church has a good testimony. They are very doctrinally sound believers. They know their scripture, and they know their Lord. There are churches that are very fond of scripture. They understand it and can preach a great message. However, they also don’t follow their own preaching. They preach down to everyone else rather than preach to themselves first. If you are a pastor reading this today, you will have to conflict with agreeing with me here. You perhaps don’t mean to do it, but you know you have in one instance or another. Instead of preaching what you’ve been inflicted by, you have preached to either inflict others or to alleviate yourself. That is not a good method of preaching. Perhaps there is a place for that, but I don’t think it’s to your congregation as frequently as possible."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul has had his fair share of preaching against the church culture several times through his letters. He has made his distaste for their actions very clear. He is able to preach against their actions in those areas because he has been on that side of the fence before. He knows what it’s like to live in the flesh. He knows what it’s like to have a culture constantly attacking you while you are living by faith. He knows what it’s like to try and be swayed away from his beliefs because it would just be easier on him if he did. That is why he can preach so strongly against it. It’s a battle he has had to face himself. It’s something that even Jesus had to face as well!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For we have not an high priest which cannot be touched with the feeling of our infirmities; but was in all points tempted like as we are, yet without sin.","ref":"Hebrews 4:15"},{"kind":"para","text":"That is an important reminder to us. Jesus faced the trials of this world and yet never fell into sin. We have fallen into sin, yet we have the ability to live a life like Christ. We can repent and abstain from sin. Christ has given us the freedom for that."},{"kind":"para","text":"This church has felt the pains and the success of Paul because they have been faithful to the Lord’s calling. They have taken Paul’s advice on the matter and have kept close to their scriptures and calling. I also think that Paul’s prayers were a gift to them to continue so strongly as well, but that could be a conversation for a different time."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When we make prayers, God records them. Perhaps He doesn't write them down on paper, but He has a memory for them. He takes them into account, and He does not forget them. Make your prayers count, because He is your record."}]},{"start":9,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This church had a love for something very specific. Paul makes mention of it right here. They loved knowledge and judgment. They had the proper knowledge of Christ and knew how to judge what was good and what was evil. That is a good thing for a church to have. Again, it’s not the only thing a church should have."},{"kind":"para","text":"Imagine a church that is quite knowledgeable in scripture and knows how to combat and debate evil people who try to sway people away from the truth. That is a biblically solid church. But imagine them having that ability and not having grace and peace when they do it. That distorts the picture of the church, doesn’t it? It makes them skillful in battle, but only to inflict pain rather than defend themselves. They are going out of their way to hurt people rather than teach people. There are far too many Christians with this mindset today."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have talked about the fruit of the Holy Spirit in several settings now, and I don't want to get too deep into it through this book. It's best saved for Galatians. But if you want a reminder, this is what the \"fruits of righteousness\" are:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, longsuffering, gentleness, goodness, faith, Meekness, temperance: against such there is no law.","ref":"Galatians 5:22-23"},{"kind":"para","text":"Notice that there is a very specific way in which we should conduct our conversation. This is a useful tool when speaking to unbelievers. We must have a conversation with them with developed fruits. If we are speaking to them without these characteristics, we will miss the chance to actually grab their attention. We must be aware of God’s Spiritual fruit developing in the conversation in order for it to be… well… fruitful. We can only develop these fruits through Christ alone."},{"kind":"para","text":"Maybe this church in Philippi knew the fruits of righteousness, and that is why Paul doesn’t hang on it too much. They knew exactly what they were supposed to be doing. Paul understands that they are doing well in most areas of the church. They are on fire for the Lord. The fruits of “righteousness” and the fruit of the “Spirit” should walk hand-in-hand. Sadly, they do not. Some have fruits of righteousness and are able to battle people with it. But we will notice that the fruit of the Spirit isn’t meant for battle alone. The fruit of the Spirit is a demonstration of grace produced in your speech. Every part of the fruit is a specific part of grace-filled speech. Without one part of it, we might not be graceful in our tact."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":14,"title":"Bonds to Boldness","entries":[{"start":12,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a fascinating thought that Paul illustrates here. There might have been some in the church at Philippi who thought Paul’s bonds were scary enough to keep them from being as bold as they should be for the faith. They didn’t want to end up in jail with Paul because they were preaching the truth. It made them think that the gospel was no longer going to be as effective (prison doesn’t make the gospel more flattering by any means). Yet, Paul says, “Being in prison has actually made the gospel further reaching!” People are seeing the way in which Paul is sold out for the faith, and they are assured that he is legitimate about what he is preaching. He is not willing to waver even though the whole Roman government is against him. He says, “My prison stint is making other preachers BOLDER to speak the truth.” That is what faithful men in times of trial should be doing. They should remain faithful so that others who see their conduct can feel more confident in their faith and preaching."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sadly, I think there are a lot of people who desire to be put on trial for their faith. They are really looking to be the face of someone on trial, instead of it just flowing naturally. They like the idea of going to prison for their faith, and they might do some borderline illegal things to get there. That is not the way in which we should do it. You don’t see Paul, or Peter, or John, or any of the other disciples purposefully trying to do things to get themselves in jail. In fact, they don’t try to do anything at all to get on their leaders’ radars. They want to avoid that. But the gospel is loud, and that can’t be shut up (even if you jail the preacher). They (the disciples) specifically speak about the importance of obeying the leaders over you specifically for that cause. They tell everyone to submit to them because they ultimately have the authority. We have a lot of preachers today who are not willing to take that advice because they want to be some sort of “Christian hero,” but in the end, it just takes glory away from the Lord by acting like that."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul’s grace and peace got him imprisoned, not his boldness to stand against the authority of his day. It’s the same with all the Christian reformers and martyrs. They are not heroes because they sought out to be one; they are heroes because they were innocent people just trying to tell the truth and were killed because of it (this includes Martin Luther, and that’s certainly something we could study a different time). If you are going against leaders without grace and peace, you are going against them by your own will, and that is not of the Lord."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":17,"title":"Types of Preaching","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Can I just remind you of what was said earlier? If you are a pastor reading this and you read my comment earlier about preaching for vindication, and you completely disagreed with it, can you please look at what Paul says here? I am just as guilty of preaching that way myself. It sometimes just falls that way, and that’s okay. We have room to correct, grow, and change (even in our preaching style and habits). We must learn to ignore the current problems and address the place in which the Lord has you. It is a difficult thing to separate personal feelings towards others from personal revelation from the Lord. It is possible, though."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul says that there are some preachers who preach CHRIST (specifically) with envy and strife. I could argue that this is the exact opposite of grace and peace. That is what a good majority of people preach in today. That is how they lead their congregation. They lead it by envy and strife. They probably don’t even mean to, but that is ultimately what happens, and it’s something that will be a problem brought up several times over in this letter."},{"kind":"para","text":"This church has a lot of great things going for it, and it’s a great model of what a church should be, but they are lacking in a very important venture for a church here, and that is in their unity through grace and peace. You don’t have to believe that this is what Paul is getting at yet, but you will come to see as we break down this letter that Paul addresses it over and over again. If it’s repeated, then it’s probably for a reason. Paul wants them to change from an envy and strife ministry to a grace and peace ministry."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, they are preaching Christ, but they are preaching Him in contention. I've been under contention preaching before, and it's not fun. It's a true message, but it's not a good message. It leaves you with a pit in your stomach. You go home, out of the church doors, feeling worse than when you came in. Everyone, including the Christians, feels weaker and saddened by the message instead of strengthened and prepared. These are like the \"friends\" of Job. They are not helpful other than to point out my flaws and make me feel even worse than I did before."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Then you have some loving preachers. You have preachers like Timothy who have stuck by Paul’s side even during his times of prison ministry. Timothy never lost his boldness in the face of Paul’s imprisonment. It made Timothy more compassionate for this sin-sick, wicked, and lost world. Just like God’s grace abounds where sin is, our grace should abound as well for the sinner."},{"kind":"para","text":"I want you to take a moment to evaluate these three preaching types today. You are one of them every time you open your mouth. Are you preaching to someone because you are envious of them, or because you are “bold” to cause strife (or conflict)? Are you preaching to them to try and put them down and be contentious towards them? Or are you preaching to them out of love and a desire for their soul’s eternity? If it’s one of the first two, may I suggest that you change up your preaching style? It will benefit you, and others, if you are doing it strictly out of love and a desire to build others up."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":20,"title":"Christ Be Magnified","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a funny verse to me. Paul says, “Even though people are preaching against me, or because they want to be like me, or because they love me, at least Christ is being preached.” He rejoices even in the meager and sorry excuses of calloused and unlearned preachers because they are at least preaching Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will see (in Paul’s other letters, as well as in Revelation) that he still desires them to preach in an appropriate way. It’s great that they preach Christ, but they must also learn better ways of doing it. That will have to be something they learn over time (if they are willing, if they are not, then they might be like the church in Ephesus, but that (again) is a conversation for a different time)."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There is power in prayer. The Bible highlights very specific things throughout, and one of those specificities is prayer. God loves it when His people come to Him and speak to Him. Even if you are not asking anything, God loves to have a conversation with His children. Paul knows the importance of prayer, and it can make the difference between pain and healing."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe that if the world was filled with prayer, everything would become possible. “Oh, this person has cancer? Everyone must pray for him.” And that person’s cancer would be gone. “Oh, this person is on his deathbed? Let’s all pray for him.” And that person would continue to live. “Oh, this person is in prison? Get the world’s prayers for him.” And he would be released due to his righteousness. You say, “Well, God wouldn’t do that; why would He answer prayers like that when it seems like He hardly answers little prayers today?” Because there are not a lot of righteous people in this world, and there is certainly not enough righteous people praying."},{"kind":"para","text":"If the whole world were righteously praying, we would see miracle after miracle. We would put the world’s healthcare system to ruin due to prayer. We will, in fact, see a world that is just like that when God has made all things new. Paul believes that God will answer their prayers and he will be released from prison if they are willing to pray for him."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That should be a desire of every child of God. Will your life magnify Christ? How about your death? We should want Christ to be magnified no matter what we are doing or what situation we face. That should be our ultimate thought whenever we are faced with an extreme burden. We should think, \"How can Christ get the glory in this situation?\""}]}]},{"start":21,"end":26,"title":"To Die Is Gain","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Pardon me for allowing my depressing thoughts to dive in here a bit. I have totally and utterly related to Paul in this situation more times than I would like to admit. I have a reason to live, no doubt, but I also have a reason to die. If I die, I lose nothing and gain everything. If I live, I gain nothing and lose everything."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For whosoever will save his life shall lose it: and whosoever will lose his life for my sake shall find it.","ref":"Matthew 16:25"},{"kind":"para","text":"However, in both situations, Christ is the center. Paul continues this thought..."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"My life (lived in the flesh) holds no value. If I live for Christ now, I actually have a responsibility and a value that is eternal. It would give me great reward to live for Christ rather than live for myself. This next thought is really where the rubber meets the road."}]},{"start":23,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul says, \"I'm in-between a rock and a hard place...\" The choices between life and death are both valuable when you have Christ on both sides. I have suffered from depression in the past, and have always wondered what it would be like if I took my life. At this juncture of my walk with Christ, I would like to say that those thoughts have completely gone away. I can’t say that though. While they have largely gone away, they still float in the air sometimes. I know that I could never take my life (it belongs to Christ, and there is no taking it back from Him), but I often wonder what it would be like to just be in eternity already. I think to myself, \"Perhaps today the Lord is done with me. Perhaps I will fall asleep tonight and wake up in eternity. Perhaps a bus might strike me going to check the mail, and I will be face to face with the Lord.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe Paul was a bit of a Stoic if I'm honest. That was the religion of the day. They thought often about death. But that was all they thought about. Paul thought about life as well and the value of it. I don't like to consider myself a Stoic, because I have value in life and I understand that value. But truthfully, I think about death and the afterlife quite a bit. To Paul, life and death were both rewarding. He says, \"I have a choice between the two, and death seems so much better because I can be with Christ already! However, the reason I am alive is because YOU need me.\" That is a good reason for any of us to live."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you struggle with depression today, may I remind you that there are other people who need you? Yes, it seems quite a dismal and never-ending process to consistently pour out to people, but they absolutely need you. They cannot do it without you. They will be completely lost and undone without your direction and presence. That is what Paul comes to realize, and that is what we must realize as well. You are here for the purpose of serving Christ, and serving others, and others certainly need that. They need your direction and edification. While I have breath in my lungs, I will seek to give it to others."}]},{"start":25,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We all have room to grow in joy. The church at Philippi may not have had much joy knowing that Paul was in prison. They probably didn’t think that this whole Christianity thing might be worth it if it just leads to imprisonment. They questioned Paul’s situation, and Paul makes it clear that this will not stop the gospel, and it should not stop their boldness to preach the faith of Christ, and it CERTAINLY should not dwindle their joy in faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"The older I get in my Christian walk, the more I realize the importance of safeguarding specific things. There are a lot of people who have faith today, but they have no joy in it. I have been in those shoes, and how miserable it is to walk in them. They are IN the faith, and they cannot reject the legitimacy of the Lord, but they cannot do any ministry with joy. They see how bleak and bothersome everything is, and so they do ministry without joy, and without love. They have no grace, they have no peace. They have no fruit in their ministering. They might even see someone come to Christ and still feel nothing for them because they have lost all joy in their faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"We must seek to refresh and renew our joy. Refresh and renew our faith. Refresh and renew our grace and peace. Renew our fruits. Take some time to be refreshed. How long that might take differs from person to person, but we all must take the time to do it, otherwise it will all be joyless and sad."}]}]},{"start":27,"end":30,"title":"A New Life","entries":[{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are some preachers of Christ who are not very graceful and kind in their message. They are not graceful and peaceful ministers. They are preachers of Christ, nevertheless, but their conversation has not “become the gospel of Christ.” “Conversation” could literally mean the things they say, but the Greek definition of the word means “behavior as a citizen.” We are citizens of Heaven when we have Christ in our life, and so our behavior should reflect that. You don’t want to move to another country and try to fit your American customs into it. Rather, you will have to adapt and change your way of living to reflect their way of living."},{"kind":"para","text":"I learned that from Texas. They are like another country compared to California. There was no way I could even speak about California things without being treated as lesser than them. There’s even a debate about In-N-Out and What-A-Burger. If I tried to make California customs ordinary in Texas, no one else would stand with me. They would all be against me, and I would be very much alone and mistreated."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul says, “If you are a child of God, and you are a citizen of Heaven, then your behavior must change to match it.” If your behavior doesn’t change, you are likely not a citizen. Paul will eventually hear about their affairs (obviously he does because he is currently writing a letter to them about their affairs right here), and he wants them to grow in their faith. That is where grace and peace come back in. The church will not have “one spirit,” or “one mind,” or be able to “strive together” without those two prominent characteristics of the faith. This church at Philippi had a lot going for it. They were on fire for the Lord. They knew how to preach the gospel. They knew what the truth was and they knew how to hold to Christ. They, however, did not have much unity. They didn’t have the ability to strive together with “one spirit and one mind.” They needed to grow in their grace and peace, not just with the Lord, but with one another."},{"kind":"para","text":"Take note. We must learn to do the same if we are going to have a unified body of believers. Don’t just be another statistic of a church with no grace and peace. Don’t be like the other twenty-eight churches that the Apostles had to write a letter to, condemning them of their flagrance of faith."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"People outside of the faith will say all sorts of things against those in the church. They will call us names, say we are wasting our time, and make fun of us for sticking to the simple account of the gospel. They will hate us because we love one another and build each other up. They won’t like that you are preaching the truth. None of that should stop us from staying consistent."},{"kind":"para","text":"We don’t have to do big things for the church to be successful; you just have to be continuous in what God has called you to do. Sometimes that might seem repetitive and mundane, but when you have a group of believers striving together with one mind and one spirit, it helps us all to feel renewed for those same repetitive tasks. That way, when those on the outside see your work for the Lord, they can say all they want, but you know you have brothers in the faith who are still by your side. What do those outside of the faith know about grace and peace anyway? They know nothing about it, and so their opinions should not be able to sway us away from the truth but make us more solidified in the love of God."}]},{"start":29,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"To suffer for Christ is not something that should be appealing to us. It is, however, something we should be preparing for. The church is in great error to not warn its congregants of the fact that they will likely suffer if they serve the Lord. I take it as a vital responsibility to let others know (those who have joined the faith and want to get involved with ministry) that there is a lot of suffering involved in that process."},{"kind":"para","text":"It requires us to seek joy and grow in grace and peace. If we are not able to do that, we will quit ministry quicker than we started."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":30}],"verses":{"1":"Paul and Timotheus, the servants of Jesus Christ, to all the saints in Christ Jesus which are at Philippi, with the bishops and deacons:","2":"Grace be unto you, and peace, from God our Father, and from the Lord Jesus Christ.","3":"I thank my God upon every remembrance of you,","4":"Always in every prayer of mine for you all making request with joy,","5":"For your fellowship in the gospel from the first day until now;","6":"Being confident of this very thing, that he which hath begun a good work in you will perform it until the day of Jesus Christ:","7":"Even as it is meet for me to think this of you all, because I have you in my heart; inasmuch as both in my bonds, and in the defence and confirmation of the gospel, ye all are partakers of my grace.","8":"For God is my record, how greatly I long after you all in the bowels of Jesus Christ.","9":"And this I pray, that your love may abound yet more and more in knowledge and in all judgment;","10":"That ye may approve things that are excellent; that ye may be sincere and without offence till the day of Christ.","11":"Being filled with the fruits of righteousness, which are by Jesus Christ, unto the glory and praise of God.","12":"But I would ye should understand, brethren, that the things which happened unto me have fallen out rather unto the furtherance of the gospel;","13":"So that my bonds in Christ are manifest in all the palace, and in all other places;","14":"And many of the brethren in the Lord, waxing confident by my bonds, are much more bold to speak the word without fear.","15":"Some indeed preach Christ even of envy and strife; and some also of good will:","16":"The one preach Christ of contention, not sincerely, supposing to add affliction to my bonds:","17":"But the other of love, knowing that I am set for the defence of the gospel.","18":"What then? notwithstanding, every way, whether in pretence, or in truth, Christ is preached; and I therein do rejoice, yea, and will rejoice.","19":"For I know that this shall turn to my salvation through your prayer, and the supply of the Spirit of Jesus Christ,","20":"According to my earnest expectation and my hope, that in nothing I shall be ashamed, but that with all boldness, as always, so now also Christ shall be magnified in my body, whether it be by life, or by death.","21":"For to me to live is Christ, and to die is gain.","22":"But if I live in the flesh, this is the fruit of my labour: yet what I shall choose I wot not.","23":"For I am in a strait betwixt two, having a desire to depart, and to be with Christ; which is far better:","24":"Nevertheless to abide in the flesh is more needful for you.","25":"And having this confidence, I know that I shall abide and continue with you all for your furtherance and joy of faith;","26":"That your rejoicing may be more abundant in Jesus Christ for me by my coming to you again.","27":"Only let your conversation be as it becometh the gospel of Christ: that whether I come and see you, or else be absent, I may hear of your affairs, that ye stand fast in one spirit, with one mind striving together for the faith of the gospel;","28":"And in nothing terrified by your adversaries: which is to them an evident token of perdition, but to you of salvation, and that of God.","29":"For unto you it is given in the behalf of Christ, not only to believe on him, but also to suffer for his sake;","30":"Having the same conflict which ye saw in me, and now hear to be in me."}},
    "2": {"chapter":2,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 02"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Paul opens this letter to the church at Philippi reminding them of the importance of staying unified in the faith. He has a lot of very kind things to say about this church, and I think he is genuine in his prayers for them and desire for them to stay the course. I assume this church has done a lot of things right, but there are some things that need to be strengthened, and their church leader knows that something needs to happen."},{"kind":"para","text":"We see in this chapter why Paul is writing to this church, and we get the different mind sets of the individual people. Christ should be our ultimate mindset to follow, and when we have the mind of Christ, we are able to function our work, efforts, and ministries in a better direction than if we try and do it through our own strength or ideas."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Paul opens this letter to the church at Philippi, reminding them of the importance of staying unified in the faith. He has a lot of very kind things to say about this church, and I think he is genuine in his prayers for them and desire for them to stay the course. I assume this church has done a lot of things right, but there are some things that need to be strengthened, and their church leader knows that something needs to happen. We see in this chapter why Paul is writing to this church, and we get the different mind sets of the individual people. Christ should be our ultimate mindset to follow, and when we have the mind of Christ, we are able to function our work, efforts, and ministries in a better direction than if we try and do it through our own strength or ideas."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":11,"title":"The Mind of Christ","entries":[{"start":1,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, I truly believe this church had a difficult time paying attention to each other. If Paul has to bring up the importance of loving one another and being in unity more than once, then it is likely for a reason. Paul is imprisoned at this time, and his focus is not on his own circumstances, but rather on the circumstances of this church. I imagine that Paul would love to be in fellowship with the churches over being in prison. He probably misses it at this moment. They are blessed to have the opportunity to be around other believers and love on one another, whereas Paul is on house arrest in Rome."},{"kind":"para","text":"“Consolation” simply means “encouragement.” If Christ has encouraged you, then it is sufficient that you should encourage others in Christ. If he has comforted you, you should pour out comfort to others. If you have fellowship with the Spirit, then that Spirit should lead you to fellowship with others. We have all received mercy, and that is reason for us to spread mercy to those around us. While all of these characteristics deserve their time in deep study, I think mercy is an important factor that gets overlooked often in church ministry. I know a lot of people who are looking for mercy."},{"kind":"para","text":"They struggle with this sin and that sin, and instead of us pointing out how terrible they are for falling victim to their sins, they want someone who will sit by them and have “bowels of mercy.” Someone who will say, “Hey. I’m so sorry you are going through this. Just know that God loves you, and I love you, and I want to help you overcome this obstacle.” There are plenty of judgmental people in this world, but not many merciful people."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For he shall have judgment without mercy, that hath shewed no mercy; and mercy rejoiceth against judgment.","ref":"James 2:13"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Be ye therefore merciful, as your Father also is merciful.","ref":"Luke 6:36"},{"kind":"para","text":"If it comes from the mouth of Christ, you can count it as a command. We are not just told to be merciful whenever it suits our needs. We are commissioned to be merciful to everyone around us as much as possible."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But go ye and learn what that meaneth, I will have mercy, and not sacrifice: for I am not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance.","ref":"Matthew 9:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"Mercy should be our song because God has shown us mercy when we did not deserve it, and it would behoove us to show others mercy when they feel they don’t deserve it either. Can we be that person? Can we be someone who can sit down with others and feel their pain? We don’t always have to give them a clear-cut answer to their problem, but we can certainly sit with them and say, “I’m hurting with you.” Some (if not most) people need that. They need someone who is simply merciful."},{"kind":"para","text":"The theme of Paul’s letter to this church is unification. He wants the whole church to be in this same mindset. Why? Is it because that is a striking good mindset to have? Does it somehow paint the church in a better light if we act this way or that way? Is it just the right Christian thing to do? No. It’s far deeper than any of that…"}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Christians are notorious for having a “humility contest.” We really try our hardest to pass the buck. Someone may compliment us for our efforts and labor in the church, and we don’t want to hang onto that thankfulness. We have an overwhelming feeling of pride when someone shows gratitude for something we have done, and (as a Christian) we want to get rid of that feeling as quickly as possible. We might say, “Thank you so much for serving! You did a great job!” The other Christian will say, “Praise the Lord, thank you for letting me serve.” That’s passing the thankfulness back to the other person. Instead of just being content with the thank you, we have to pass it off."},{"kind":"para","text":"The truth of it is, when we serve (at least it should be this way), we serve with the reality that we are lifting others up above ourselves. We want others to grow closer to God, rather than bring ourselves closer to God. Perhaps the people we lift up are those that don’t know how to grow deeper in their relationship to the Lord? Maybe they are new to all of this and they need someone to lift them further. This does not negate the other person’s responsibility to lift others up as well. If we are lifting others up who are not willing to lift others up, then we should probably seek to place others in those positions because we are setting people up for a prideful fall."},{"kind":"para","text":"It should be, in the church, that everyone has a turn to be lifted up by others. We should all be in the house of moving each other forward. That is sadly not the case in most churches, and I believe it was not the case in the church of Philippi. Someone always ends up getting left behind. Perhaps the one left behind for this church is Paul. Paul is still receiving word about how this church is growing and serving, but they are skeptical of his bonds and imprisonment. They are starting to shy away from Paul’s teaching. Perhaps that is why they are starting to become disorganized and disunionized? It shouldn’t matter how good the pastor looks of a church in order for people to move forward. If we are moving forward (singularly as a people with the pastor’s prompt to commission us), the faith continues on based on the person of Christ, not the leadership of the church. If we are simply following church leadership for direction, and we don’t have a personal direction from Christ, we will fail the moment our leadership does (and I have no doubt that our church leadership will fail)."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, if everyone is minding their efforts in lifting others up, there should be no one left behind. If someone gets left behind, it’s because there are some people not looking on “the things of others.” They are focusing on themselves, their own efforts, or they are focused on how great it feels to be lifted up, and they leave behind the person that was lifting them to begin with. I don’t want to move forward if I cannot bring those that have supported me with me. I want them all to be equals with me. I don’t like the idea of a hierarchy."},{"kind":"para","text":"I was teaching in the Bible study the other day, and a new man came into our group. He said, “Are you the leader?” I’m usually not at a loss for words. I sat there for a moment trying to figure out the best way to address that question and I said, “I am the one that keeps the study organized, yes.” His response was, “I mean, are you the one that puts this together? You don’t have to be weird about it.” That was a funny thought to me. “Yes,” I said, “I suppose that makes me the leader, it’s just a weird way to think about it because there are people here who are far more scriptural and well-studied than me.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"The truth is (though I may be listed as the life group leader), I see all these great men of faith in our church as equals to me. I don’t want to put myself above them. I want them to be leaders WITH me. Someone once said, “Leaders make leaders.” That is the goal, and the best way to do that is if we are willing to put others before ourselves who are willing to put others before THEMselves. How do we pick those people? Well, Paul actually has an answer for us:"}]},{"start":5,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Christ displayed humility and servant-leadership. That was His mindset. He was able to become a leader simply by bringing people with Him and serving them. His disciples were equals with Him, yet He was still their leader. They were people flawed in nearly every area, but Christ was their guide and compass. He reminded them about what to look for when faced with distractions of all sorts. He was their constant."},{"kind":"para","text":"Christ’s humility was displayed in an ultimate way on the cross. He did not go to that cross to die for Himself. He didn’t do it because it was mandated and the government was forcing it. In fact, Christ could have talked His way out of being given over to be crucified. Pilate literally gives Him the opportunity to stand up for Himself, and He just stays quiet."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And went again into the judgment hall, and saith unto Jesus, Whence art thou? But Jesus gave him no answer. Then saith Pilate unto him, Speakest thou not unto me? knowest thou not that I have power to crucify thee, and have power to release thee? Jesus answered, Thou couldest have no power at all against me, except it were given thee from above: therefore he that delivered me unto thee hath the greater sin. And from thenceforth Pilate sought to release him: but the Jews cried out, saying, If thou let this man go, thou art not Caesar's friend: whosoever maketh himself a king speaketh against Caesar.","ref":"John 19:9-12"},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus knew what was going to happen to Him. He purposely says nothing because it means dying for the sins of the world. He has the opportunity to save humanity, and that is exactly what He did by going to the cross. He did it for others. He minded the things of others when sacrificing Himself. He literally sacrificed His own life so that others could live theirs. Is that not the ultimate sign of humility? That is what Paul is relating here."},{"kind":"para","text":"We should sacrifice our own lives to lift up the lives of others. We should be willing to sacrifice our own time to save the time for others. And it should work the other way around too. Those people whom we are sacrificing for should be willing to sacrifice themselves for us. That is how the relationship of the Lord works. We want to sacrifice our lives and time for the Lord because we love Him so much that we don’t see any other way to serve Him. Yet we can’t seem to do that with others in the faith. We find it easy to give our time and efforts to the Lord, but we don’t find many causes to give our time and efforts to others. Arguably, serving the Lord should be the same as serving others. That is how we serve the Lord, because that is the example that the Lord showed us in His service. That is Christ’s mind. His mind is that He puts others first before Himself."}]},{"start":9,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These verses truly are powerful and wonderful. His name is higher than any other name. He was humble and lowly, yet His name is lifted high! We were just speaking about how we take God for granted the other day. While the disciples were with Christ, they took Him for granted. They waited until His death to finally realize how valuable He was in their lives. They waited until His ascension before they started to live out His legacy. That is (sadly) what will likely happen with any of us who are putting in the effort now to build God’s house on Earth. We will be taken for granted. We take the Lord for granted every day."},{"kind":"para","text":"We (I hope) know that God is with us, and that He is there for us, and He is personable. Yet, we have reason to forsake that and try to do things on our own. We don’t always look to Him and think, “Wow! The creator of the universe actually wants to spend time with me? What a privilege!” No, we are usually so preoccupied with our own necessities that we forsake time spent with the Lord. We know He’s there, but we don’t have that awe of how personable He is to us. We take His presence for granted. We should be living with this awe in mind every moment of every day. His name is powerful, His presence is personable, His will is responsible, His love is exceptional, His commitment is faithful, His direction is achievable, and His truth is valuable. By all standards, then, our life should be useable, our reliance pliable, our wills reliable, our care expectant, our trust strengthened, our following resilient, and our minds in-tuned to His. His mind is the one above all else, and we must come before Him with the desire and goal of taking HIS mind and replacing OUR minds with it. We need to have the mind of Christ dwelling in us if we are going to have a church focused on grace and peace. There is no unity in the church if there are people who don’t have the mind of Christ dwelling in them."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":18,"title":"The Mind of Paul","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We can look at this verse at face value and just assume that when Paul says “salvation,” he is speaking about their faith walks in general (and perhaps there are unbelievers who need to learn the faith). However, by the context of the rest of this chapter (and letter), I believe Paul is stating that they are to work out their “own salvation” in the sense of priorities. He considers getting out of jail a form of “salvation,” as spoken about in the last chapter. So the trials they might face would be something they would have to work out for their freedom in the future."},{"kind":"para","text":"They will be persecuted. Paul was being persecuted as he sat in prison, and Timothy will be persecuted as he helps Paul. Paul is really stating (here) that his imprisonment should give them boldness to “work out” their own freedom with fear and trembling. In other words, they should take it extremely seriously and do it with care. They will likely suffer persecution, and Paul knows that, but they can do it together if they are caring enough and prepared enough. They need to work it out together, though. Otherwise, every man will be for himself, and most people will faint and become weary."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This church knows the Lord, and there is no doubt in Paul’s mind. This verse is here to remind them of that. They understand that they are only standing (and have a church at all) simply because the Lord has provided for them so far. They are given their power and privilege straight from the Lord themselves. That is a gift to them from the Lord. It is a reminder to us as well. We should be willing to go to great lengths in our faith, only because we are assured and confident that God is working in us. It’s His will and desire, and He can do whatever pleases Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe that is why Paul started this letter off with the occasion of life and death. He let it be known that if it’s his time to die, that is up to the Lord. The Lord knows what is right and what to do, and it’s God’s “good pleasure” that will move the tides where they may go."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If I can be honest, I have done some work in the Lord with murmurings and disputing. I have questioned the reason we do some things. Perhaps it’s my sinful nature. I want to get involved, and when I am given the opportunity, I am told to do it in a specific way that just doesn’t make sense to me. I think (sometimes out loud), “Why are we doing it this way when it would be far easier and smarter to do it that way?” I have a bit of a rebellious side to me. I try to kill that side as much as possible, but stupidity brings out my sinful rebellious nature. It is wise for us to kill that side of us. As soon as it rears its head, we should suffocate it quickly."},{"kind":"para","text":"If we want to do ministry, we must seek to do it without murmurings and disputing. This is another verse insinuating that the church at Philippi had problems in their unity in Christ."}]},{"start":15,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There is nothing worse than when Christians attack each other. I see it all the time online. Instead of privately messaging someone, they post comments on posts for the whole world to see and debate other Christians about their theology and doctrine. There is no more time for people to pull others to the side and have a conversation with them. Everyone’s thoughts and ideas are out for the world to see, and when the world sees two Christians attacking each other, they laugh and say, “I knew they were a bunch of hypocrites. They can’t even get along with each other, and they expect me to accept their ‘truth’?”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Satan is satisfied when Christians fight each other. We must avoid murmurings and disputing so we can be “blameless and harmless” as the “sons of God” should be. God has a Son (Jesus Christ). Can you point out one time that Jesus did something with murmuring or disputing? I had a preacher read this verse off to me to dispute (ironically enough) this question:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then Jesus answered and said, O faithless and perverse generation, how long shall I be with you? how long shall I suffer you? bring him hither to me.","ref":"Matthew 17:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"When we read this verse at face value, we are under the impression that Jesus is murmuring a little bit here. We can read it as if He is tired of having to always fix these people’s problems. We read it and think, “Gee, Jesus was growing quite impatient in His ministry. If He didn’t want to help people, why did He come to Earth and help people?” That’s a great question! Except Jesus is not murmuring here. He is not growing impatient in this verse. He is relating the fact that they were not willing to listen to His teaching and says, “You know, there will be a time I am not here, and you must take these problems to yourselves right?” He was preparing the disciples for His death so they knew how to navigate these problems when He wasn’t there to guide them. His Holy Spirit will eventually guide them, but that would only happen through a reliance on that Spirit."},{"kind":"para","text":"At the time, they were specifically relying on Jesus, and they couldn’t take their own strength and practices of faith to heart. They had the physical Jesus, why would they need to rely on a spiritual God? But Jesus’ example was to point everything back to God, even in that instance. So there can be no time traced to Jesus murmuring or disputing with people. He was simply pleased to serve, and in so doing we have an example to follow. We can have His mindset, and we can be lights in a very dark and cruel world."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is what Christ was. He was (and still is) a light in the middle of a crooked and perverse world. Better yet, we become the “sons of God.” That’s plural. More than one. We can all have our own lights to bear, and when you have a group of lights together it becomes… a party? Well, I will just say it makes things a whole lot brighter."}]},{"start":17,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul wants to see this church succeed in the midst of his trial. He does not want his efforts for this church to simply stop because he is gone or dead. He wants them to continue forward in faith by the Will of God, not by the presence of Paul. That is important for the church to take note of today."},{"kind":"para","text":"If your pastor were to die today, would the church continue forward? I can’t think of a single church where this would be true. Churches very heavily rely on their pastors, and (sadly) when a new pastor comes in, they change the rules and start throwing out some useful ministries, which will lead to a decline and downfall of their church. Pastors should be preparing the church to continue without them, and churches should be preparing their pastors to set them up for success. That is Paul’s vision for this church at Philippi. He wants them to treat this trial as a gift from the Lord. It’s a means for them to cling a little tighter to their freedom to preach and teach because it can be taken away at any given moment. It’s a reminder to the church that they still have the ability to love one another and unite with the body while they can because there is a great chance that it will be very difficult (if not impossible) for them to do later."}]}]},{"start":19,"end":24,"title":"The Mind of Timothy","entries":[{"start":19,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, we have Paul preparing the church for his absence, or possibly his death, as he isn’t sure what will happen next. The best way he could think of preparing this church is to send his most trusted minister to them. He is sending Timothy. Paul loves Timothy, and while I don’t think I need to dwell on that fact too much because we have spoken about this in previous letters and chapters and will likely speak more about it in later letters as well, it is important to note. He trusts Timothy because Timothy is likeminded to Paul. They have the same ministry goals and dreams. They want to serve and build churches."},{"kind":"para","text":"He knows that Timothy will put them in the proper mindset to continue forward in the church and organize unity for them to work together. When Timothy comes back to Paul, it will either be because he has helped the church unite, or the church has rejected the message and has fallen into disarray. Timothy will be a leader builder. He will go to the leadership of this church at Philippi and give them some resources and help to navigate their growing problem of disunity."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is such a painful verse. There are a lot of ministers that Paul was around. He could probably list off a great number of people who would be more than capable of visiting this church and building them up. But he says here, “There are no other ministers who are willing to take the cause of Christ. They all seek after their own things, and their own churches, and they don’t care to help out anyone else.” It’s another lesson to the church of Philippi."},{"kind":"para","text":"They were (likely) in disunity, and the reason for it is they weren’t seeking after the mind of Christ. They were seeking after their own dreams and goals. Timothy was the only one (it seems) by the side of Paul at this moment. Luke was likely there too, so I’m assuming that he doesn’t refer to Luke simply because Luke had his hands tied in another matter. It is also possible that the other Apostles were imprisoned and going through their own hardships, so that just leaves a remnant of people, and that small remnant wasn’t concerned about helping a good church remain the course. They wanted to go help their own churches that they personally started."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think there are a lot of pastors and church leaders today who feel very much like Paul does in this moment. The only difference is, they are not in a literal prison. They are imprisoned to their own minds, and so they feel like they are completely alone, and no one has the same mind as them. I (personally) may not have the same mind as my pastor, but I do have a small glimpse of his hope. I can use that small hope to build a part in his mind. If every member of the church (especially the leaders) are able to place their small nugget of hope to strengthen the mind of the pastor, we could all have the unity that is needed to support the church ministry. Perhaps not everyone is a great soulwinner, but there are people who are, and they can place their small ability as a strengthening tool. Not everyone is a great teacher, but there are people who are, and they can place their small ability as a strengthening tool. Not everyone is a great planner, but there are people who are, and they can place their small ability as a strengthening tool. My point is, everyone should be doing their small part so that the ministry flows easier."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now I want to focus more on the pastors here (because that is really where Paul is headed; he wants the leadership to be on track, and it helps the rest of the people follow suit). The pastors should not have the mindset that the congregation should do what the pastors want in the way the pastors want them done. You will not succeed in that battle. We should simply be thankful that there are people doing something at all, even if it’s not in the way you envisioned it to be done. Leaders have to give up control on how things are done if we want unity and growth. You won’t see that happen in a lot of leadership, however, and this is ultimately why things become fractured and chaotic in the church house today. Sending Timothy is Paul’s way of letting his grip on this church go. He is trusting that Timothy will do what is right by this church because Paul cannot physically be with them like he wants to."}]},{"start":22,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul cares deeply about Timothy. If you are a leader, no matter the ministry, in your church, you should be looking for a Timothy. You must find someone you can build up in the faith and prepare for when you are no longer able to control the ministry. Someone who can either take over or alleviate your burdens. Someone who is able to have a like-minded vision for the future goals of the ministry and the church. It’s a difficult thing to find, but God will provide someone."},{"kind":"para","text":"Personally, I seek after the person who is least likely to be looked at as a leader. Those are the ones that I want to pour most of my time into. If I don’t see them as a leader, then they are perfect for the job."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul still wants to make it there. He is hopeful that he does not die in this prison in Rome. He is hopeful that when he makes it out (if the Lord allows him), he will come to this church personally and pray with them and help them. He WANTS to be hands-on. I think that is vital to understand as a leader. Just because you are delegating responsibilities does not mean you should just give up all presence for those ministries so someone else can flourish. Rather, we should still desire to be hands-on and personal with others as well. Let go of the ministry, but don’t walk away from it. What a difficult balance to make, and many leaders have failed."}]}]},{"start":25,"end":30,"title":"The Mind of Epaphroditus","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Epaphroditus was a man that Paul trusted as well. You might say, “Well now hang on. Paul said that he had no one likeminded to send to them, and yet he has just labeled two very specific people whom he trusts to send to them. Which is it then? Does he have no one, or does he have an assortment?” I believe Paul had plenty more than he realizes he has, but they are not all reachable to him at the moment. The only person he had in his presence at this time was Timothy and Epaphroditus. Epaphroditus was already at the church in Philippi. He likely came with the letter from the church stating what they were facing. Obviously, he is the church’s messenger (Paul literally says that in this verse), and it seems that when he gets to Paul, he has all his ducks in line."},{"kind":"para","text":"I assume that when Paul started the church, Epaphroditus was sent to be their pastor, and he has stayed the course for this long (doing a fantastic job, mind you). He is evaluating the problem with unity in the church and says, “I don’t know how to fix this. Maybe I send a letter to Paul and see what his thoughts are.” Well, here are Paul’s thoughts. His mind is that Epaphroditus would have the mind of Christ, and Timothy (who currently has the mind of Christ) will go back with him and remind the church about that mindset (that’s a bit of a tongue twister)."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that Epaphroditus becomes sick, though the exact sickness is unknown. The church at Philippi hears about his condition. He was a man who played a significant role in starting this church, and as a result, they love him dearly and are deeply hurt by his illness. They have great compassion for him."},{"kind":"para","text":"After coming this way to visit Paul and deliver the questions of the church, Epaphroditus becomes sick and is unable to return home."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think the Lord made this intentional. There is nothing that makes a man more desirous to do something than when he is no longer capable of doing it. If I were stuck in a hospital bed with the desire to preach and told to rest because I am very ill, it would be like torture to me. I would be preaching to the people around me because I have to let it out. This man was so severely sick that he almost died, and so the church prays for him, and Paul prays for him, and it seems that the Lord has mercy on Epaphroditus and heals him. Now he’s on fire for the Lord and can be sent back to Philippi to serve the church that cared for his health."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is also nothing like a severely sick leader that brings the church together. I think the church will be very different by the time Epaphroditus comes back. He will have word about unity, but they will also already be in that mindset because of their necessity to unite in prayer for their sick leader. 28 I sent him therefore the more carefully, that, when ye see him again, ye may rejoice, and that I may be the less sorrowful."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul wants him to take it easy on his way back. However, he knows the lengths and suffering he’s had to endure to take good care of this church. Epaphroditus loves this church in Philippi. He is willing to go to great lengths for them to ensure they are taken care of and that things are flowing smoothly. He knows the words of Paul are solid and that he would provide good direction for the saints. Therefore, that is the first place he wants to go, even if Paul is in the middle of a prison sentence. Thankfully, as we’ve studied in the book of Acts, Paul has special rights while he is imprisoned in Rome."},{"kind":"para","text":"29 Receive him therefore in the Lord with all gladness; and hold such in reputation: 30 Because for the work of Christ he was nigh unto death, not regarding his life, to supply your lack of service toward me."},{"kind":"para","text":"This was a praise, commission, and a stab all wrapped up into one thought. They should hold this man high because of his faithfulness to care for this church enough to put his own life on the line. The church (perhaps) was disunited, but this man was not willing to let that just slide by. He wanted to do something about it, and seeing as he didn’t have all the information, he went to someone who had seen a thing or two in his lifetime. He went to great lengths to show his care for his church, and it would be a terribly sad thing to find out that they don’t take recognition or care for him because of it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Further, Paul says, “The reason he went to such great lengths was for his love for you, even though you are not willing to stand by me in my trials.” This points out another reason why I think disunity is the problem in this church. They see Paul’s imprisonment and reject their calling because they are scared that they will end up the same way. Paul says, “You might. But that is not a good reason to stop being faithful people.” There is no good reason to stop being faithful to the Lord. If there is anyone who claims Christ and has decommissioned their faith because of some trial or another, they are not doing it for good reasons (you can bank on that). They are doing it because they have very evil and sinful intentions to do it. They want something they shouldn’t have. That is the only reason someone will reject their faithful lifestyle."},{"kind":"para","text":"This church could be viewed by the same standard. They are rejecting their faith not because they are fearful of imprisonment. Rather, they are rejecting their faith because they are not willing to change their lifestyles to grace and peace. They like their envy and strife. It’s easier to live with that mindset. Honestly, it really does take work to live in grace and peace, but it’s a work that is worth the effort because it causes a greater impact and better growth (spiritually and numerically) if we exercise it."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":27}],"verses":{"1":"If there be therefore any consolation in Christ, if any comfort of love, if any fellowship of the Spirit, if any bowels and mercies,","2":"Fulfil ye my joy, that ye be likeminded, having the same love, being of one accord, of one mind.","3":"Let nothing be done through strife or vainglory; but in lowliness of mind let each esteem other better than themselves.","4":"Look not every man on his own things, but every man also on the things of others.","5":"Let this mind be in you, which was also in Christ Jesus:","6":"Who, being in the form of God, thought it not robbery to be equal with God:","7":"But made himself of no reputation, and took upon him the form of a servant, and was made in the likeness of men:","8":"And being found in fashion as a man, he humbled himself, and became obedient unto death, even the death of the cross.","9":"Wherefore God also hath highly exalted him, and given him a name which is above every name:","10":"That at the name of Jesus every knee should bow, of things in heaven, and things in earth, and things under the earth;","11":"And that every tongue should confess that Jesus Christ is Lord, to the glory of God the Father.","12":"Wherefore, my beloved, as ye have always obeyed, not as in my presence only, but now much more in my absence, work out your own salvation with fear and trembling.","13":"For it is God which worketh in you both to will and to do of his good pleasure.","14":"Do all things without murmurings and disputings:","15":"That ye may be blameless and harmless, the sons of God, without rebuke, in the midst of a crooked and perverse nation, among whom ye shine as lights in the world;","16":"Holding forth the word of life; that I may rejoice in the day of Christ, that I have not run in vain, neither laboured in vain.","17":"Yea, and if I be offered upon the sacrifice and service of your faith, I joy, and rejoice with you all.","18":"For the same cause also do ye joy, and rejoice with me.","19":"But I trust in the Lord Jesus to send Timotheus shortly unto you, that I also may be of good comfort, when I know your state.","20":"For I have no man likeminded, who will naturally care for your state.","21":"For all seek their own, not the things which are Jesus Christ's.","22":"But ye know the proof of him, that, as a son with the father, he hath served with me in the gospel.","23":"Him therefore I hope to send presently, so soon as I shall see how it will go with me.","24":"But I trust in the Lord that I also myself shall come shortly.","25":"Yet I supposed it necessary to send to you Epaphroditus, my brother, and companion in labour, and fellowsoldier, but your messenger, and he that ministered to my wants.","26":"For he longed after you all, and was full of heaviness, because that ye had heard that he had been sick.","27":"For indeed he was sick nigh unto death: but God had mercy on him; and not on him only, but on me also, lest I should have sorrow upon sorrow."}},
    "3": {"chapter":3,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 03"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Paul spoke about the mind that Christ has, and how it should impermiate our own thoughts. It's something we should all (individually) be striving for so we can all (as a group) work together like a well-oiled machine. He demonstrates his own mind, and the minds of his brothers in Christ, and demonstrates (by their unity) what the mind of Christ can provide if we are exercised by it."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Paul illustrates the importance of keeping our eyes fixed on Christ, keeping His mind in us as we travel through life, and how that will lead us to a rejoicing spirit in the Lord."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Paul spoke about the mind that Christ has, and how it should permeate our own thoughts. It's something we should all (individually) be striving for so we can all (as a group) work together like a well-oiled machine. He demonstrates his own mind, and the minds of his brothers in Christ, and demonstrates (by their unity) what the mind of Christ can provide if we are exercised by it. In this chapter, Paul illustrates the importance of keeping our eyes fixed on Christ, keeping His mind in us as we travel through life, and how that will lead us to a rejoicing spirit in the Lord."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":1,"title":"Rejoice in the Lord","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I love it when Paul says, “Finally.” We assume that he is going to finish up what he is speaking about, but really he continues on for quite a bit more time. This is how you can know it’s Paul who is writing this letter. He never actually means “finally” when he says it. There are a lot of preachers who say that when they are preaching. They spend a half an hour speaking and preaching scripture and they say, “lastly and we are almost finished” just to spend thirty more minutes finishing up their sermon. It makes me chuckle."},{"kind":"para","text":"But this final thought is a key one if we want to see grace and peace in our churches today. He says, “rejoice in the Lord.” This will not be the last time that Paul will tell this church to remember this…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Rejoice in the Lord alway: and again I say, Rejoice.","ref":"Philippians 4:4"},{"kind":"para","text":"He tells them to rejoice here, and then he reminds them two more times in the next chapter. We must take this into consideration, and I speak about it more in the next chapter, but rejoicing is something we must NEVER overlook or forsake. Yet, it’s something we casually forget about. Paul is writing to this church in a “safe” way. In other words, he feels comfortable being able to give them the truth flat out because they were mature enough to take it. There were other churches he had to casually bring up their flaws in and build up to the moment of correction, but with the Philippians, he could be upfront and personal."},{"kind":"para","text":"They were a church on fire for the Lord, there is no doubt about that, but there was a bit of division and a bit of stale work happening. Churches just get to that point sometimes. I know I do. I have gotten to points where I become stale in my ministering. Doing the same thing over and over again week in and week out can become a bit drab. Add on top of the fact that there is no one else in the church leadership willing to help you, and you will start to feel stretched quite thin. That is what the Philippian church is facing. They needed the mind of Christ, and that mind was able to “rejoice” in the Lord."}]}]},{"start":2,"end":3,"title":"Be Aware","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are a number of people in the church who are desiring to serve the Lord and love others. However, there are a great deal more (and I am just being honest here) who are serving with the intent of hurting others and serving themselves. I have yet to find a church that doesn’t have this problem. Sadly, this problem resides largely in the leadership of the church. They are all seeking out for their own (like Paul had mentioned in the last chapter). They don’t really care about the ministry like they should, and so they are like dogs, or evil workers, or concision seekers. They want to hurt people, not help people. Whether it’s intentional or not, I cannot say. But from those that I’ve had to deal with in these situations, it certainly seems like they are doing it on purpose."},{"kind":"para","text":"We actually have three different types of people labeled here. We have “dogs.” There are people today who are actually dressing like dogs and acting like dogs. They are believing they are literal dogs. I don’t think that is what Paul had in mind when he wrote this verse out. I think he was speaking about workers who were acting like dogs."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"His watchmen are blind: they are all ignorant, they are all dumb dogs, they cannot bark; sleeping, lying down, loving to slumber.","ref":"Isaiah 56:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"These are lazy people. They are individuals who desire recognition for their actions but are unwilling to put in the effort to earn it. They are often referred to as “consumer” Christians. They attend services, watch a sermon, greet a few people, and then leave without getting involved or genuinely loving other Christians. They are closely related to evil workers."},{"kind":"para","text":"Evil workers are those who engage in ministry for personal gain. They seek to extract something from you rather than invest in you. There are many self-proclaimed “ministers” who fit this description. They do not have your best interests at heart but are eager to be involved in your drama. It is crucial to be cautious about whom you share your personal matters with. These individuals are closely related to concisions."},{"kind":"para","text":"“Concision” means “to cut up” or “to mutilate.” These are people who seek to harm you. Paul warns the church to be vigilant about these individuals because they may be right next to them, potentially working alongside them. This could be a significant reason for the disunity within their church. They have likely been hurt by people they trusted, making it easier to rely on themselves rather than others. This is perhaps why most ministers, even today, struggle to trust others in ministry."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are people who will purposely cut you up (the concision), and then there are people who bond with your affliction (the circumcision). The circumcision is a reference to being saved and separated for the Lord. Those who have the Spirit of worship, are more than capable of rejoicing in Christ, and are willing to reject their own flesh are those who are trusted Christians. You can look at both of these lists and point out where you stand. At least (in theory) it shouldn’t be that hard to look in the mirror of your soul and see if you are a problem maker or a problem solver. Perhaps that is difficult for some people to see, but it really shouldn’t be for those who know Christ. We should know where we stand with Him."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":16,"title":"Press Towards Christ","entries":[{"start":4,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is what a religious life looks like. Paul lived that life once. He says that he was more zealous than the most religious man. I know of a lot of religious men today. Some of them even go to my church. There are a good majority in my family who are quite religious. Maybe we compare them to Paul here. Perhaps these religious men were literally and physically circumcised. We might even pretend for a second that they are from the lineage of Abraham. Paul was from the tribe of Benjamin. He was a lawful man and followed strict orders just like the rest of the Pharisees. He certainly wasn’t a Sadducee because he believed there would be a resurrection of the dead. He had zeal, so much so that he was willing to persecute other churches that were not teaching what he knew to be true. That is what my family would do today."},{"kind":"para","text":"They would talk about how much they hate the doctrine of my church because it doesn’t line up with their religion. But that’s just the Bible that’s not lining up with them. Paul understands that now, but he thought just like my religious family. He considered himself “blameless” by the standards of his religion. How about you today? Do you consider yourself blameless? If you do, why? Is it because Christ has made you blameless, or your priest has made you blameless? Paul thought the same way you do today if you are entrenched in religion. But look at what he says next now that he is a saved man…"}]},{"start":7,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The next two verses go with this, but I want to break up this section quickly because Paul is saying some very important things here. All of his religious practices and his life spent on becoming the most religious man this world has ever seen amounted to nothing more than “loss” to Paul. He came to know Christ, and it didn’t matter what his church was teaching him or what the wisest scholars had to say anymore. It was all about getting to know Jesus more. The scholars wouldn’t talk about Jesus nearly as much as they talked about the law or holy living. The priests and “teachers” talked solemnly about how to stay on God’s good side. That is still the problem with religions today. They don’t talk about Jesus. They talk about God’s law and about how we should work for the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul finally came to terms with the fact that if he wanted to know Christ deeper, he had to get rid of his religious practices. He was told that it was all about how righteous a life he could live, but he now realizes that none of that even matters when we have the Lord. We don’t live in righteousness to stay on God’s good side. We can only be on God’s good side if we have Christ. He is the only way we can come to God to begin with. So Paul says we all have a choice. We can either have our own righteousness (which is by the law) and completely reject the Lord and His goodness altogether… OR we can have faith in Christ, which will be what makes us righteous (without the works of the law)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Knowing that a man is not justified by the works of the law, but by the faith of Jesus Christ, even we have believed in Jesus Christ, that we might be justified by the faith of Christ, and not by the works of the law: for by the works of the law shall no flesh be justified.","ref":"Galatians 2:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"Just know you can only choose one. You may not have both. But if you choose the path of self-righteousness, you will miss out on some amazing blessings that only Christ can provide. Paul illustrates what he gave up religion for next..."}]},{"start":10,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He continues in a moment, but what an amazing thing for us to take in. Paul was willing to give up everything he spent his whole life learning to get to know the power of Christ, His fellowship (and presence with us), and His peace (that is what it means to be conformable to His death). We have a peace for eternity. If you are not willing to take Christ, you will never have a peace for your eternity. If you are not willing to rid your religion of you, you will not have Christ's presence with you. You can either take the presence of your religion, or the presence of Christ. To know Christ's power and resurrection is the most assured thing you can have in this life. There is nothing like trusting the Lord for His sacrifice."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, you might read this and think, “Yeah! I don’t know what he said, but I agree with it!” This will seem a little like a word puzzle at first glance. We will see that Paul doesn’t just leave the conversation here, but he clarifies in the next couple of verses. But the gist of it (to set us up for what he is about to say next) is that Paul is fully aware that he is not perfect. He does not have perfect knowledge and has not become some sort of superhuman that can articulate every single aspect of the Lord and His sovereign plan. That’s a little funny to me because there are many religions that think quite highly of Paul, yet Paul does not think highly of himself in any of his letters. Actually, he is very blatant about recognizing his hang-ups and pointing out his errors and sinful nature. He was not a perfect man, and he wants everyone to know it. He does not want people to treat him as a sinless man, but rather as a growing man. That is what he will say next."}]},{"start":13,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He has left his religious practices behind because he sees Christ ahead. It is worth giving up everything to pursue Christ. He will never fully apprehend (that is, he will never fully understand and be perfect), but he is pursuing the one that is perfect. Every step closer he takes to the Lord, a little more understanding he receives. That is what it looks like to be a truly born-again Christian. We are stepping closer to the Lord, and further away from this world. You will never fully be there until your death, but that is the direction we are going in. We are looking up, while the world looks down."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let us pray that we know how we are minded. Do we have the mind of Christ, or a different mind? I think we could evaluate ourselves properly by this small letter to see if we have the proper mindset. Of course, he is not saying that there are any that are “perfect” in the sense of being without sin, or with full knowledge. No, he literally just explained how he is not either of those things (and he has a far better testimony than most in the church of his day). Rather, “perfect” here means “mature.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"We should all be maturing in Christ. If we are not maturing, we will not be useful. If we stay still, we will not mature. We must be growing in the Lord. I heard about a young man who was not maturing. He was maybe five or six years old, and he could barely walk on his own. He was in school, but his parents didn’t want the school to teach him anything or help him grow. He didn’t know how to talk, and he wasn’t allowed to eat anything except baby food. He was not maturing. He was not growing. The longer he is kept from walking, being taught to talk, and being allowed to eat, the more difficult it will be for him to grow."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are a lot of Christians like this today. They are either baby Christians too afraid to ripen, or they are mature Christians too afraid to continue growing. There are some who have fallen off the tree altogether honestly. We should just trust the Lord when He directs us, and allow Him to grow us. It might be a little difficult, but that is what a good healthy church is for. It’s there to help us grow together in the Lord."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let’s all walk together. Let’s get out there and press towards the same mark. We can help each other grow if we are willing to go out there and take a chance on the Lord. If we are willing to leave our mistakes, our sins, our religions, our traditions, and anything else that draws us away from the Lord behind, and simply follow after Christ, we will have growth in our lives. We have to be willing to take the risk, though. We have to be willing to say, “You know what, I may not believe everything, but I am willing to see where this goes, and keep an open mind. I am willing to learn with those that are also willing to learn.” That is the only way for people to be saved. They have to be willing to question things and willing to take the answers that are given, searching out the truth and seeing if it’s correct. They have to give up those things that they think are important and hold on to Christ alone."},{"kind":"para","text":"My daughter made a huge mess in the living room (she does this often), and we had told her to clean it up before we leave. We had to head out the door in a few minutes, so she was scrambling to clean it all up. She grabbed a bunch of her stuff (papers and other things) and her cup of milk and everything all at once. You could probably guess how that turned out. We are trying to hold onto everything we have, thinking that they will all help us in some way if we just add this to our holding of Christ. The problem is, we can’t carry everything. We will eventually have to drop something. Sadly, those that think this way simply let go of Christ. They don’t find value in Him as much as they do these other strange traditions and religious practices. They think their family’s lineage and passed-down knowledge is far more important than pressing towards Christ. What a terribly sad thought…"}]}]},{"start":17,"end":21,"title":"Fix Your Eyes","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul presented three minds in our last chapter, four if we include Christ. These three minds—those of Paul, Timothy, and Epaphroditus—were striving to adopt the mind of Christ, which brought them into unity. Paul was essentially saying, “Follow me, because we are following the Lord. You can use our example in this area.” While there were many areas where Paul might have advised against taking their example, his followership of Christ was not one of them. He wanted others to follow Christ with the same intensity and fervency that he did."},{"kind":"para","text":"Can you say the same about your own faith? Paul was imprisoned for following Christ with such fervency. Are you willing to go to that length to follow Christ? While it is hoped that such a situation is avoided, it is not entirely impossible in today’s world to be shunned and mistreated for your faith. People are frequently persecuted for following Christ today. Can you follow Paul’s example in following Christ? If not, you will not have unity with your church, even if the entire church agrees not to follow Christ. It is only Christ alone who provides unity."}]},{"start":18,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They “walk.” This means they are on the course with you, perhaps even pushing you forward, but they are not doing it to give you encouragement. They have an ulterior motive. They have a plan for what they want to do with you once they help you forward. This makes Paul sad to the point of literally and physically “weeping.” I imagine he weeps because there were people he thought he could trust well who turned out to be people who abused his ministry and friendship. You are, sadly, going to run into that in ministry."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are a lot of people who will not move you forward other than to get something out of you. They will lead you to destruction, and they are looking to feed their own appetites. All they care about is the earthly things, the personal glory. In fact, they want to rob God from having any glory. Be careful of people like this. But there will be people (though they may be few) who will actually be there for you. It is all dependent upon their faith in the Lord. That is where we must look when we are heading forward. We must look to the Lord. If the people simply followed Paul, they would all be destined to fail. Paul will eventually die, and this church will have no one else to follow if all they depended upon was Paul. That is why Paul tells them to follow the Lord in the same manner. They should be alongside Paul, not behind him."}]},{"start":20,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Think on eternity and what is to come from the Lord. Do not dwell on things behind you or the trials around you. Focus on your life for the Lord. We have a home in Heaven, and that is what every single one of us should be focused on. However, this does not mean we should ignore the pains and trials of others here on Earth. It does not relinquish our duties as Christians to reach the lost with the gospel. Rather, it gives us hope and joy when we do these other things."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we have our eyes, minds, and hearts fixed on eternity, it gives us a joyful purpose to continue with things here on Earth. That won’t always be easy, but it will always be necessary. I don’t know how people live without the Lord. Before I was saved, I had no purpose in my life. Life was just something you do until death, and if you were lucky (in my eyes), death would come quickly. Now I realize that if death came as quickly as I wanted, I would have been a man in eternal torment and torture for all of eternity. Life is worth living because the Lord has given it to me. Before I knew the Lord, I was already dead. Now that I know Him, I have life and a direction to go. I gave up my thoughts, hopes, and dreams because He became everything I wanted and needed. That is the thought (I believe) of every true Christian. They seek after the Lord and His glory. They will help others along in that trek and will desire that people grow. If you are around people who are not helping you move forward, then you are likely around enemies of the cross, dogs, evil workers, and the concision. Be aware of those around you, but also don’t take your eyes off of eternity. I could argue, honestly, that if we have our eyes focused on Heaven, we can rightfully and joyfully serve people around us properly because of it."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":21}],"verses":{"1":"Finally, my brethren, rejoice in the Lord. To write the same things to you, to me indeed is not grievous, but for you it is safe.","2":"Beware of dogs, beware of evil workers, beware of the concision.","3":"For we are the circumcision, which worship God in the spirit, and rejoice in Christ Jesus, and have no confidence in the flesh.","4":"Though I might also have confidence in the flesh. If any other man thinketh that he hath whereof he might trust in the flesh, I more:","5":"Circumcised the eighth day, of the stock of Israel, of the tribe of Benjamin, an Hebrew of the Hebrews; as touching the law, a Pharisee;","6":"Concerning zeal, persecuting the church; touching the righteousness which is in the law, blameless.","7":"But what things were gain to me, those I counted loss for Christ.","8":"Yea doubtless, and I count all things but loss for the excellency of the knowledge of Christ Jesus my Lord: for whom I have suffered the loss of all things, and do count them but dung, that I may win Christ,","9":"And be found in him, not having mine own righteousness, which is of the law, but that which is through the faith of Christ, the righteousness which is of God by faith:","10":"That I may know him, and the power of his resurrection, and the fellowship of his sufferings, being made conformable unto his death;","11":"If by any means I might attain unto the resurrection of the dead.","12":"Not as though I had already attained, either were already perfect: but I follow after, if that I may apprehend that for which also I am apprehended of Christ Jesus.","13":"Brethren, I count not myself to have apprehended: but this one thing I do, forgetting those things which are behind, and reaching forth unto those things which are before,","14":"I press toward the mark for the prize of the high calling of God in Christ Jesus.","15":"Let us therefore, as many as be perfect, be thus minded: and if in any thing ye be otherwise minded, God shall reveal even this unto you.","16":"Nevertheless, whereto we have already attained, let us walk by the same rule, let us mind the same thing.","17":"Brethren, be followers together of me, and mark them which walk so as ye have us for an ensample.","18":"(For many walk, of whom I have told you often, and now tell you even weeping, that they are the enemies of the cross of Christ:","19":"Whose end is destruction, whose God is their belly, and whose glory is in their shame, who mind earthly things.)","20":"For our conversation is in heaven; from whence also we look for the Saviour, the Lord Jesus Christ:","21":"Who shall change our vile body, that it may be fashioned like unto his glorious body, according to the working whereby he is able even to subdue all things unto himself."}},
    "4": {"chapter":4,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 04"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Paul talks strongly about the importance of the church keeping their eyes fixed on eternity. They, of course, were not supposed to forget their labor of love towards those that are around them, but they also needed their minds and eyes focused on the right things in order for their outpouring to be effectual in the church. If we don't have our eyes fixed appropriately, we won't have our ministries working accordingly."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Paul finishes up this letter to the church with a very special message (some small instructions) and how to maintain a rejoicing spirit and heart in the Lord."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, Paul talks strongly about the importance of the church keeping their eyes fixed on eternity. They (of course) were not supposed to forget their labor of love towards those that are around them, but they also needed their minds and eyes focused on the right things in order for their outpouring to be effectual in the church. If we don't have our eyes fixed appropriately, we won't have our ministries working accordingly. In this chapter, Paul finishes up this letter to the church with a very special message (some small instructions) and how to maintain a rejoicing spirit and heart in the Lord."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":1,"title":"A Beloved Church","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a very kind sentiment that Paul is giving here. These are strong words from the Apostle. If it wasn’t obvious enough, you can tell that Paul seriously cares very much about this church. They are beloved, “longed for,” and his “crown” and “joy.” What a testament to this church! How do you feel about your church home? Is it like Paul feels about this church? I find it hard to believe that there would be many in my own church house who feel this way about their church. I don’t even know if I am always feeling this way. I’d say the majority of the time I feel this way, but not always."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul is excited about this church’s conduct and willingness to grow and change, and that is why he is longing for them. There are several other churches he is looking forward to going back to visit, but nothing compared to this church. Remember, the emphasis of this letter is to keep them in mind of Christ. That is the ultimate unifying factor for them."}]}]},{"start":2,"end":3,"title":"A Factor of Disunity","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we have two more minds added to the mix. “Euodias” means “fragrant,” and “Syntyche” means “with fate.” Their names seem to complement each other. However, these two ladies appear to have a problem. How deep that problem is, I don’t know. They don’t seem to be of “the same mind in the Lord,” and so Paul makes brief mention of them here."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will sometimes come into positions in ministry where we have differing opinions about certain things, and that is okay. It’s okay not to agree with everything your pastor says. I think it’s silly that a pastor would surround himself with yes-men and agreeable people. He does not look for the challenging ones. He doesn’t look for people who will question him. He just wants those who will do as they are told and nothing more. That’s a danger to his own growth and leadership. We should want people around us who can challenge us. They shouldn’t be mean when they challenge, just concerned and thoughtful. These ladies were challenging each other, and it seems they may not have had a very good time ministering together."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have another name. “Clement” means “mild” or “merciful.” This Clement person must have been present when the church was started and these ladies were becoming ministers. These ladies were prominent teachers in the church. They were leaders in their own right, but they had some divisive ideas about certain things. Paul is requesting that the church address them, encourage them, and help them meet each other in the middle. That is a challenging task with differing opinions. It’s even harder for a church to coax them to speak and discuss their problems. It will often lead to churches choosing sides, and that is not what we want to happen at all. It’s not good for a church to choose sides. It’s also not good for a church to just ignore problems when they arise. They should be involved in finding a solution, not just letting it go and hoping that it works itself out. The situation doesn’t seem to be too dire, however, so Paul leaves it for this last part here, and it’s a very brief portion of this letter. But it could escalate into something bigger if they don’t take care of it now."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the problem I see in our churches today. Little problems don’t stay little forever. Perhaps that is why Paul makes it a point to talk about disunity quite a bit in this letter. Maybe they are somewhat of an example as to why the church is not working together as strongly as they should. If they take Paul’s advice on this, they can unite together to unite these ladies. That will be enough unity to suffice for Paul’s pleading with this church here."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":7,"title":"Careful For Nothing","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Don’t forget about how much the Lord has done for you. We have a lot to rejoice about, and when we are a unified church, we have greater purpose to rejoice. Honestly, unity provides us with the Spirit of rejoicing. If we are not finding joy in our ministry, you will usually be able to trace that back to some sort of disunity in the body. Paul makes it clear that rejoicing is something that we must not forget. He even repeats himself, almost like a plea."},{"kind":"para","text":"Boy, it’s hard to dwell in God’s joy. We live in an ever-darkening world, and it is hard to find room to rejoice. But the Lord is the one that provides us with joy. He is the one that gives us reason to rejoice. The reason many of us can’t do it is because we have our minds set on the world around us, when we should have (as Paul says) the minds of Christ. Even for the cross, Christ rejoiced."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Looking unto Jesus the author and finisher of our faith; who for the joy that was set before him endured the cross, despising the shame, and is set down at the right hand of the throne of God.","ref":"Hebrews 12:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"He rejoiced in His own painful death because it meant the salvation of the world. He had His eyes fixed on Heaven, and His mind set on grace and peace. That is where all of us need to be individually, and then collectively in the church house."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Moderation” means “suitable” or “equitable.” It’s to be fitted and reasonable. We are to be suitable and well-fitted in our conduct and conversation. We can only be suited correctly if we are in Christ, though. That is what we must remember when we are doing any sort of work in this life. Christ is our suitor. When we are living in conduct like this, we are doing it not because we think it looks better and feels better. We are doing it because we know it pleases the Lord, and there could be any moment that the Lord returns."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul says He “is at hand.” In other words, he’s here. He’s coming to you now. Don’t treat it like the Lord is coming in the future, but look at it as if He is anticipating His coming today (this very moment even)! That has always been a wonder to me. What would I be doing in the middle of Christ’s return? Prayerfully something that pleases the Lord, but He will call His people together, and none of us will be prepared. We might be spiritually prepared, but it will physically take us by surprise. We should treat every moment of our lives in anticipation of the Lord to call us home. That is a great thing to think about. He is not just coming in the future; He is here with you today. Take that as you may see fit."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a great verse, and I like to go back to it from time to time and think on it (along with the verses that are about to come in a moment). Paul is not saying we should just simply be careless about our lives. He’s not saying we should risk everything for something unsure. No. He’s saying we should not worry about things that we think deserve to be worried about."},{"kind":"para","text":"We often worry about this or that getting done today, but if the Lord returns, you won’t be getting it done anyway. You should pay attention to those things that are important for the now. What would be something that is important if the Lord returned today? If you were to be raptured, what is one thing you believe needs to get done before you go? That answer is probably “nothing.” We see that all of it will vanish away anyway."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And God shall wipe away all tears from their eyes; and there shall be no more death, neither sorrow, nor crying, neither shall there be any more pain: for the former things are passed away.","ref":"Revelation 21:4"},{"kind":"para","text":"So we must be careful for nothing. Yet, in EVERYTHING, we should be praying and thanking the Lord. While we are here, we must pray and supply. That is our duty as men. D.L. Moody says (and it’s my favorite of his quotes) “Careful for nothing. Prayerful for everything. Thankful for anything.\""}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God's peace will keep us, because we are not worried about the things we can lose. Everything must belong to Him anyway. In fact, if there is something you think is valuable enough to keep, place it in His hands. He will do what is best with it. Let's not pass by the fact that Paul is bringing peace back into the picture. I have said that grace and peace are the theme of this letter, and he is not going to let it go that easily. When we have our minds on Jesus, we have our peace."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":9,"title":"Think on These Things","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In our bothersome and worry-filled lives, we think about a great deal of things. We think about the bills that are due, the chores that are piling up, and our families whom we have to reach out to. These are all fine and well, but they should not be our focus. Paul already advised us not to worry about anything. However, here, Paul is giving us things that we SHOULD be thinking about. He says, “Think about things that are true, honest, just, pure, lovely, and of good report.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Why should we think on these things? Well, it brings us into rejoicing, as Paul has mentioned, and it will provide us with grace and peace. This is also the way in which Jesus minded. We all know that there is a lot of evil and wickedness in this world. That is why Jesus came to Earth. He was the embodiment of this list of good things."},{"kind":"para","text":"He is true."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Jesus saith unto him, I am the way, the truth, and the life: no man cometh unto the Father, but by me.","ref":"John 14:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"He is honest."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But let your communication be, Yea, yea; Nay, nay: for whatsoever is more than these cometh of evil.","ref":"Matthew 5:37"},{"kind":"para","text":"He is just."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For Christ also hath once suffered for sins, the just for the unjust, that he might bring us to God, being put to death in the flesh, but quickened by the Spirit:","ref":"1 Peter 3:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"He is pure."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Blessed are the pure in heart: for they shall see God.","ref":"Matthew 5:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"He is lovely."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Teaching them to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you: and, lo, I am with you alway, even unto the end of the world. Amen.","ref":"Matthew 28:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"He has a good report. The four gospels are sufficient enough evidence of this, plus adding on the testimony of Paul, as well as the moving of the church. Therefore, the rightful thing to think on (always, at all times) is Jesus Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"But let’s make this a little more personal here. We can always look at all situations and see the bad in them. Let’s take our bills being due for a moment. That is usually not associated with good thoughts, let’s be honest. We could think of it, however, in the fact that we have a house at all to pay for. Some people today can’t afford that (with the economy the way it is). We could also think to ourselves, “I’m so glad that the Lord has provided shelter for me, and a family to love and take care of. These things are lovely and true in my life.” Bills will still come, but you don’t have to worry about them when you think on the Lord. He is someone that will take care of your needs when your thoughts are on Him."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, Paul brings up peace. God is the author and publisher of peace. This world will never have peace without God. You know why the hippy movement didn’t go much further outside of the 70s? Well, it’s because they had peace and love without the Lord. If the Lord is not in it, it won’t last. The Lord was evidently with Paul, and so his lifestyle was a learning lesson for everyone following. The things that he did right were examples for others to do as well. The buck doesn’t stop with Paul."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":20,"title":"Thankful to the Church","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Paul is thankful for this church. It seems that they desired to talk to him sooner, as indicated by his words, “ye were also careful,” but they couldn’t seem to get ahold of him, which is why he says, “but ye lacked opportunity.” He had been in prison, making it impossible for them to reach him. They didn’t have an opportunity to share the ministry’s successes and failures with him. However, he is thankful that they have found him and have reached out to him. They seemed to care about Paul just as much as he cares about them."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a good lesson for us that Paul had to learn in prison. Prayerfully, we can take Paul’s prison stint as the means to follow his advice here, rather than getting ourselves into some trouble to learn this lesson the hard way. Paul has “learned” to be content with where the Lord has him. If he’s in the middle of prison, or in the middle of the ocean, or in the middle of a church, he is happy that the Lord is using him. That was probably not always his thought in life or ministry. There were times (I have no doubt) where he was frustrated, or he was confused, or he was searching for an escape, but he has learned to just be content with where the Lord leads (even if it’s in the middle of distress or uncertainty)."}]},{"start":12,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Abased” means “to bring low.” So he knows how to be brought low and also how to stay up. He knows how to be filled and yet hunger for more. He knows how to live in comfort and suffer. He can worry about nothing and pray for everything all at the same time. These are things we will all learn through our walks of faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have seen some in recent years give up one side or the other. I wonder if they were ever truly walking to begin with. But they give up, and they learn how to be hungry and never be filled again. They learn how to suffer and never how to comfort. They learn how to be brought low but never get back up. So they are living “Christian” lives in turmoil and distress. They are not living with the mind of Christ (which is found in rejoicing and contentment). Ultimately, that is what peace is. It’s contentment. It’s accepting (like Paul has) what the Lord has put into your life and rejoicing over the fact that He has put it there. That includes the pain and trials (we can talk about that in 1 & 2 Peter or James)."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Philippians understood his pain. They were not like the preachers condemning Paul in the Lord because of his imprisonment. They found Paul, and instead of condemning him, they were just thankful that he was still alive."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This church was supporting Paul even from a distance. It seems this was the only one. They were willing to give what they could to help Paul in his ministry needs. What a rare commodity today! We might give through our church to missionaries, and that is all well and fine, but we usually don’t support them because we individually love the missionary. It’s usually supported by the church leadership, and the church just follows as a whole. We should all be in agreement when giving to someone else’s work. If we cannot provide the funds, that is completely okay as well."},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul didn’t request funds from any of the churches, but the Philippians thought it was appropriate to help Paul meet his needs. He was worthy of that in their eyes."}]},{"start":16,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"All that Paul wanted was for the fruit of the gospel to be shared. He desired for people to come to know the Lord. He would gladly use up all of his resources to make it happen. He had no one else relying on him to do these things. He had no family, no commitments, nothing to hold him down or back (save the chains of his imprisonment). He could pursue Christ with everything he had, and that was just to see people saved."},{"kind":"para","text":"Are there church leaders who still care about this alone today? I don’t know. I can’t think of many. They are all after a paycheck honestly. If they stopped getting paid, many of them would likely turn away from their duties in church leadership. They would go somewhere else or do something else. That is not how ministers should be…"}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The news from Epaphroditus was enough to fill Paul up. Knowing that this church was flourishing and that they were willing to learn and grow from Paul was enough for him. It was a sweet smell to Paul."}]},{"start":19,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God will supply for the church when the church is yielded to the Lord. Again, we must give what we think is valuable to the Lord. That includes our time and family."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":23,"title":"Salute the Saints","entries":[{"start":21,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that while Paul was in Rome on house arrest, he was able to convert a few people in Caesar’s circle. We will find out that Caesar is not a very fun guy and will not take Paul’s faith seriously. In fact, he will become a terror to the Christians. However, there were plenty of people that Paul was still able to reach even though he was arrested. God has used people like this several times throughout scripture. He did it with Joseph while in prison, with the prophets who suffered persecution, and with the disciples in the New Testament. I am quite sure He still does it today with His saints in this world. We don’t hear much about it anymore, but I guarantee people’s lives are being touched by Christians who are imprisoned for their faith. They have the mind of Paul, who has the mind of Christ. They are content to be wherever the Lord would have them. That is the great takeaway from this letter. We should have grace and peace because of our mind being set on Christ and our eyes on Heaven. Don’t lose thought or sight of this."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":23}],"verses":{"1":"Therefore, my brethren dearly beloved and longed for, my joy and crown, so stand fast in the Lord, my dearly beloved.","2":"I beseech Euodias, and beseech Syntyche, that they be of the same mind in the Lord.","3":"And I intreat thee also, true yokefellow, help those women which laboured with me in the gospel, with Clement also, and with other my fellowlabourers, whose names are in the book of life.","4":"Rejoice in the Lord always: and again I say, Rejoice.","5":"Let your moderation be known unto all men. The Lord is at hand.","6":"Be careful for nothing; but in every thing by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving let your requests be made known unto God.","7":"And the peace of God, which passeth all understanding, shall keep your hearts and minds through Christ Jesus.","8":"Finally, brethren, whatsoever things are true, whatsoever things are honest, whatsoever things are just, whatsoever things are pure, whatsoever things are lovely, whatsoever things are of good report; if there be any virtue, and if there be any praise, think on these things.","9":"Those things, which ye have both learned, and received, and heard, and seen in me, do: and the God of peace shall be with you.","10":"But I rejoiced in the Lord greatly, that now at the last your care of me hath flourished again; wherein ye were also careful, but ye lacked opportunity.","11":"Not that I speak in respect of want: for I have learned, in whatsoever state I am, therewith to be content.","12":"I know both how to be abased, and I know how to abound: every where and in all things I am instructed both to be full and to be hungry, both to abound and to suffer need.","13":"I can do all things through Christ which strengtheneth me.","14":"Notwithstanding ye have well done, that ye did communicate with my affliction.","15":"Now ye Philippians know also, that in the beginning of the gospel, when I departed from Macedonia, no church communicated with me as concerning giving and receiving, but ye only.","16":"For even in Thessalonica ye sent once and again unto my necessity.","17":"Not because I desire a gift: but I desire fruit that may abound to your account.","18":"But I have all, and abound: I am full, having received of Epaphroditus the things which were sent from you, an odour of a sweet smell, a sacrifice acceptable, wellpleasing to God.","19":"But my God shall supply all your need according to his riches in glory by Christ Jesus.","20":"Now unto God and our Father be glory for ever and ever. Amen.","21":"Salute every saint in Christ Jesus. The brethren which are with me greet you.","22":"All the saints salute you, chiefly they that are of Caesar's household.","23":"The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you all. Amen."}},
  },
};
